Sort the meetings by their ending time and keep the last meeting that was accepted. Whenever the next meeting starts after that ending time, it can be added safely.

For example, meetings `(1, 2), (3, 4), (0, 6)` are processed by end time. The first two are selected, while `(0, 6)` overlaps with the first one. The comparison is strict because a meeting starting exactly when another one ends is not considered valid here.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    public:
    int maxMeetings(vector<int>& start, vector<int>& end){
        vector<pair<int, int>> meetings;
        for (int i = 0; i < start.size(); ++i) {
            meetings.push_back({end[i], start[i]});
        }

        sort(meetings.begin(), meetings.end());

        int count = 0;
        int lastEnd = -1;

        for (const auto& meeting : meetings) {
            int meetingEnd = meeting.first;
            int meetingStart = meeting.second;

            if (meetingStart > lastEnd) {
                ++count;
                lastEnd = meetingEnd;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

Sorting takes `O(n log n)` time, and the scan takes `O(n)` time. The extra space is `O(n)`. The input arrays are not changed.

This version builds the schedule from the end instead of from the beginning. After sorting by start time in decreasing order, it picks the meeting that starts latest and then looks for a meeting ending before that start.

The same rule is used in reverse: selected meetings must satisfy `end < nextStart`. If there are no meetings, the loop simply returns zero.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int maxMeetings(int[] start, int[] end) {
        int n = start.length;
        int[][] meetings = new int[n][2];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            meetings[i][0] = start[i];
            meetings[i][1] = end[i];
        }

        Arrays.sort(meetings, (a, b) -> {
            if (a[0] != b[0]) {
                return Integer.compare(b[0], a[0]);
            }
            return Integer.compare(b[1], a[1]);
        });

        int count = 0;
        int nextStart = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for (int[] meeting : meetings) {
            if (meeting[1] < nextStart) {
                count++;
                nextStart = meeting[0];
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

The arrays are copied into a local 2D array, so the original input is left unchanged. The time complexity is `O(n log n)`, and the extra space complexity is `O(n)`.

This is a slower learning version that checks both choices for each meeting: leave it out, or include it when it starts after the last selected meeting ends. Memoization avoids repeating the same take-or-skip state.

The meetings are sorted by end time only to keep the recursion easy to follow. For an empty input, the first call returns zero. This method is useful for checking the greedy result on small cases, but it is not the version to use for very large input.

```python
from functools import lru_cache

class Solution:
    def maxMeetings(self, start, end):
        meetings = sorted(zip(start, end), key=lambda meeting: meeting[1])

        @lru_cache(None)
        def search(index, last_end):
            if index == len(meetings):
                return 0

            skip = search(index + 1, last_end)
            take = 0

            meeting_start, meeting_end = meetings[index]
            if meeting_start > last_end:
                take = 1 + search(index + 1, meeting_end)

            return max(skip, take)

        return search(0, -1)
```

There can be up to `O(n^2)` useful states because `last_end` can come from many meetings. The time and space complexity are both `O(n^2)` in the worst case, not counting the sorted input copy. The original lists are unchanged.

Instead of choosing meetings from left to right, this sweep works from the right side. It always takes the meeting with the latest start that can fit before the meetings already chosen.

For instance, after selecting a meeting starting at `7`, only meetings ending before `7` are allowed next. This makes the schedule grow backward while keeping every selected pair separate.

