## Intuition
We need to sort both arrays according to End time of meetings. and check if start of current meeting doesn't overlaps with the end time of recent meeting.

## Approach
Editorial has used List as an datastructure so it would be easier to sort based on end timings which took O(n) space.

*but we can optimize this space to O(log n) by implementing custom Quick sort algorithm (takes reccursive stack space), which primarily finds pivot from end timings array, and sort it along with start timings array.*
-

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int maxMeetings(int[] start, int[] end) {
        int n = start.length;
        quickSort(start, end, 0, n-1);
        int meeting=1;
        int recentEnd = end[0];
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            if(recentEnd<start[i]){ 
                recentEnd = end[i];
                meeting++;
            }
        }
        return meeting;
    }
    public void quickSort(int[] start, int[] end, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pIndex = partition(start, end, low, high);
            quickSort(start, end, low, pIndex - 1);
            quickSort(start, end, pIndex + 1, high);
        }
    }
    public int partition(int[] start, int[] end, int low, int high){
        int pivot = end[low];
        int i = low;
        int j = high;
        while (i < j) {
            while (end[i] <= pivot && i <= high - 1)  i++;
            while (end[j] > pivot && j >= low + 1) j--;
            if (i < j)   swap(start, end, i, j);
        }
        // Place pivot in correct position
        swap(start, end, low, j);
        return j;
    }
    public void swap(int[] start, int[] end, int i, int j){
        int temp1 = start[i];
        int temp2 = end[i];

        start[i] = start[j];
        end[i] = end[j];

        start[j] = temp1;
        end[j] = temp2;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity**
O(n log n) The time complexity is dominated by the `quickSort` method. In the average case, `quickSort` takes O(n log n) time. This is because the `partition` method processes each element in a sub-array once (O(k) time), and `quickSort` performs approximately `log n` levels of recursion. The subsequent `for` loop iterates `n-1` times, performing constant-time operations (comparison, assignment, increment) in each iteration, contributing O(n) time. Therefore, the total time complexity is O(n log n) + O(n) = O(n log n).


**Space Complexity**
O(log n) The space complexity is determined primarily by the recursion stack used by the `quickSort` method. In the average case, `quickSort` has a recursion depth of O(log n) due to balanced partitioning, with each stack frame using O(1) space for local variables. All other variables (e.g., `n`, `meeting`, `recentEnd`, loop variable `i`, and temporary variables in `swap`) use a constant amount of memory, O(1). Thus, the overall space complexity is O(log n).

